Yes. I think this wants its own Pantheon-style gym:

Deal Operator Gym

The key move is not to catalogue every named contract humans have invented. There are too many. Instead, identify the small number of deal primitives from which nearly all of them can be compiled.

A mortgage, marriage covenant, insurance policy, treaty, subscription, employment agreement, option, escrow, partnership, bounty, and handshake deal all become different compositions of a common instruction set.

Legally, some of these distinctions are already quite sharp: a contract creates enforceable mutual obligations; a lease separates ownership from temporary possession/use; an option grants a right without imposing the corresponding obligation to exercise it; escrow inserts a neutral conditional custodian; insurance reallocates specified risk; guarantees, sureties, and collateral provide different forms of backstop. 

1. The universal deal object

Represent any deal as:

D=(P,V,R,O,C,T,K,E,X,M)

Where:

* P = parties
* V = value/resources
* R = rights
* O = obligations
* C = conditions/triggers
* T = time structure
* K = risk allocation
* E = enforcement/remedies
* X = exit/termination
* M = memory/record/proof

Then a deal is literally a state-transition machine:

S_{t+1}=\delta_D(S_t,event_t)

def deal(state, event):
    rights = evaluate_rights(state, event)
    obligations = evaluate_obligations(state, event)
    transfers = execute_due_transfers(state, event)
    remedies = handle_failures(state, event)
    return next_state(...)

So:

A deal is a protocol for routing value, rights, obligations, authority, risk, and consequences through time.

That’s the invariant.

