2. The primitive instruction set

These are the deeper operators.

TRANSFER

Move ownership/value.

T(A,B,x)

transfer(x, from_=A, to=B)

Kata: Who owns it before? Who owns it afterward?

Gift, sale, repayment, donation, inheritance and payment all contain this.

⸻

SWAP

Reciprocal transfer.

SWAP(A:x,\;B:y)

swap(A, x, B, y)

Basic exchange operator.

⸻

PROMISE

Create a future obligation.

O_A(\phi)

promise(A, action=phi)

This is more primitive than contract.

⸻

CONDITION

Make an operation contingent.

C\Rightarrow O

when(condition, do=action)

This single operator creates enormous complexity.

⸻

DELAY

Move performance through time.

O(t_0)\rightarrow O(t_1)

schedule(action, at=t1)

Credit is fundamentally exchange + delay.

⸻

REPEAT

Turn a transaction into a relationship.

O_t,\quad t=1,2,\ldots,n

repeat(action, every=period)

Subscription, rent, salary, royalties.

⸻

GRANT_RIGHT

Give someone permission without transferring the underlying thing.

grant(holder=B,
      right="use",
      resource=x,
      scope=s,
      expires=T)

License and lease families emerge here.

⸻

RESERVE_RIGHT

Give someone a choice about future execution.

R=\{execute,\;don't\ execute\}

option(holder=B, action=buy_x, expires=T)

An option’s essential distinction is precisely that the holder possesses the right but not the obligation to perform. 

⸻

DELEGATE

Transfer decision authority without necessarily transferring ownership.

delegate(
    principal=A,
    agent=B,
    authority=scope
)

Agency.

⸻

CUSTODY

Transfer possession without ownership.

custody(asset=x, owner=A, custodian=B)

Bailment, storage, escrow.

⸻

POOL

Combine resources or risks.

Pool=\sum_i x_i

pool(contributions)

Partnerships, insurance pools, investment funds.

⸻

SHARE

Divide outcomes according to a rule.

p_i=\alpha_i P

share(outcome, weights)

Equity, commission, royalties, partnerships.

⸻

METER

Observe usage or performance.

usage = meter(resource, metric)
payment = rate * usage

Turns flat deals into usage-based deals.

⸻

BACKSTOP

Introduce another performer if the first fails.

if not performs(A):
    perform(B)

Guarantee family.

⸻

COLLATERALIZE

Attach an asset to an obligation.

default(A)\Rightarrow claim_B(x)

secure(obligation=loan, collateral=house)

That’s the deep structure underneath secured lending; legally, collateral gives the secured party rights against specified property upon default. 

⸻

ESCROW

Conditional custody.

escrow.deposit(x)
if condition:
    escrow.release(to=B)
else:
    escrow.return_to(A)

Cornell’s legal definition is almost directly executable pseudocode: a neutral third party holds assets until specified conditions are satisfied. 

⸻

RELEASE

Destroy or surrender a right.

release(A, claim_against=B)

Waiver, release, settlement.

⸻

CONSTRAIN

Remove possible actions.

forbid(A, disclose(secret))
forbid(A, trade_with=competitors)

NDA, exclusivity, non-disclosure, territorial restrictions.

⸻

VERIFY

Use an observable predicate to determine performance.

if verify(delivery):
    pay()

This is enormously important.

The oracle problem of deals is:

Who decides whether the condition actually happened?

⸻

REMEDY

Define what happens after failure.

if breach:
    refund()
    repair()
    replace()
    compensate()
    terminate()

Without remedy, many deals are promises with weak error handling.

⸻

PRESERVE

And here is the covenant operator:

\forall S_t,\quad preserve(I)

def covenant(state):
    action = choose_action_that_preserves(INVARIANT, state)
    return action

Unlike a contract clause, it doesn’t necessarily enumerate every required action.

It provides a generator.

