21. And the failure-mode gym

Every deal should also be attacked with recurring adversaries:

Default

Someone can’t/won’t perform.

Moral hazard

The deal changes someone’s incentive after agreement.

Adverse selection

One party knows their hidden risk/type better before agreement.

Principal-agent failure

Delegated actor optimizes themselves instead of principal.

Hold-up

One party gains leverage after the other makes an irreversible investment.

Oracle failure

Nobody can reliably determine whether the trigger occurred.

Ambiguity

Two interpreters compile the same language differently.

Liquidity failure

Someone is solvent overall but cannot perform now.

Counterparty failure

Someone disappears/dies/fails.

Time inconsistency

The future version of a party no longer wants what the present version promised.

Specification gaming

metric\neq true\ objective

Someone satisfies the literal terms while defeating their purpose.

And there—contract vs covenant comes roaring back in.

A covenant can potentially resist specification gaming because it says:

preserve the invariant, not merely the metric.

But its corresponding failure mode is interpretive capture:

“I claim this behavior preserves the spirit.”

So the ideal architecture often wants both:

\boxed{Covenant\ invariant + Contract\ specification}

Spirit tells you what must survive.
Terms tell you how we’re currently trying to make it survive.

